$$
Let $t=\frac{\sin{(k\pi+\alpha)}}{\cos{(k\pi+\alpha)}}$. We have $$3t=\cos{\alpha}-3\sin{\alpha}+1 \Rightarrow \cos{\alpha}=3t+1+3\sin{\alpha} $$	\displaystyle \displaystyle \displaystyle \cos^2{\alpha}=9(t^2+1)(t+1)^2+6t(1+t)+1 \Rightarrow 10t^2+16t+6=0, \tag{$\star$}$$
where we used $\sin^2{(k\pi+\alpha)}+\cos^2{(k\pi+\alpha)}=1$, and squaring on both sides.
Next we notice that $$\tan{\alpha}=\frac{\sin{\alpha}}{\cos{\alpha}}=\frac{1-3t}{3t^2+3t+1}$$
Hence $$\tan{(k\pi+\alpha)}=\frac{1-3t}{3t^2+3t+1}, \tag{$\star{\star}$}$$
with which we can eliminate $\sin{\alpha}, \cos{\alpha}$, and obtain the expression of $f(x)=0$ with $t$ as the unknown. The interested reader can verify that we eventually get 
